    fn create_widget(&self,name:String)->impl Future<Output=Result<WidgetRow,StoreError>>+Send;
    fn delete_widget(&self,key:WidgetId)->impl Future<Output=Result<bool,StoreError>>+Send;
}
pub trait OpenWidgetStore: WidgetStorage+Sized {
    fn open(config:&OpenConfig)->impl Future<Output=Result<Self,StoreError>>+Send;
}
pub trait StoresView {
    type Widgets: WidgetStorage;
    fn widgets(&self)->&Self::Widgets;
}
```
Tsql=existing schema::WidgetRow{id:schema::WidgetId,name:String}; Key=schema::WidgetId; draft New={name:String} has no key. Keep api-types CLI DTO identities and existing conversions/errors. WidgetStorage uses the generic atomic unit/CRUD transaction primitives for generated-key insert/delete; map typed storage failures to existing StoreCode/StoreError, never strings. Each engine exports generic Store<T> and async open<T:StorageRow>(&EngineConfig)->Result<Store<T>,StorageError>; EngineConfig is table-neutral (SQLite path, Postgres URL, MySQL URL). It validates target and applies the application-supplied row schema before returning. Application-owned OpenWidgetStore translates existing OpenConfig/StoreError, not an engine trait. SQLite Store clones one supplied/shared SqliteDatabase. Generated service Stores implements StoresView and passes its Widgets to common commands. d-5 owns actual schema definitions; d-6/7/8 implement generic Storage<T>/CRUD primitives with arbitrary contract rows; d-10 implements WidgetStorage/OpenWidgetStore application wrappers and consumes them.
StorageCommands/StorageCommandError and Transform signatures from STEP3 live in sc-storage; sc-command's local DefaultStorageCommands implements that foreign trait in d-10. Factory/codec definitions live in real writer src/contract.rs and exported lib.rs; contract tests import them. SqliteDatabase::open(path,capacity,readers).await then storage::<T>(mapping).await applies registered generic schema on its writer; no consumer table code in backend. Factory/mapping declarations are frozen before consumers begin. No signature copies remain as an alternate source.
